\fs32 \cf6 \cb3 \strokec6 Example Production Workflow:\
A high-end agency producing a 60-second brand spot might use LTX Studio for storyboarding and character definition (locking brand Elements), Runway Gen-3 for rapid shot generation and motion-specific controls, Sora 2 for the cinematic hero shot requiring physical realism, and ComfyUI with custom nodes for color grading, temporal coherence management, and final upscaling to 4K  .\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs36 \cf6 \cb3 Critical Friction Points in 2025/2026\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf6 \cb3 Temporal Consistency: Despite advances, character drift remains the primary production killer. As one practitioner noted, "By clip 3, she\'92s blonde with contacts. By clip 5, she\'92s a dude" . Solutions require manual "Character DNA" prompt injection, reference image packs (6-12 images per character), and batching similar shots rather than chronological generation  .\cb1 \
\cb3 Brand Drift: Without explicit governance, AI models hallucinate brand elements\'97logos migrate, color palettes shift, and typography mutates. Current solutions rely on LTX's "Elements" system or Runway's custom model training, but these require significant upfront curation  .\cb1 \
\cb3 Prompt Complexity: "Prompt engineering" is still engineering. Agencies report spending more time refining prompts than traditional pre-production would take. The "prompt-to-pixels" gap forces creative directors to think like programmers  .\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

